result.recommendedChange:
Ship (1)+(2) and the phraseme guard. Do not ship (3) on VERB/ADJ.

1. Port prototypes/audit/e2-canonical-form/candidates.ts to src/concrete-lang/de/grammatical-resolution/headword-candidates.ts, and extend resolveGrammarJudgments in judgments.ts (the alternatives block, l.158-165). Keep stored Lemmas and NOUN members first, then append codeCanonicalCandidates(kind, members, gaps, followedByText). gaps and followedByText come from encounter.sentence.segments between and after the memberSegmentIndices. Filter out the joined candidate and cap at 64. Keep a text->source map so a later check can tell stored headwords from code guesses:
   const sources = new Map(); ...stored -> 'stored', NOUN members -> 'nounMember', code -> rule;
   const canonicalFormAlternatives = [...sources.keys()].filter(t => t !== canonicalFormCandidate).slice(0, 64);

2. After selected('canonical'), resolve the chosen text first. Then apply two guards:
   - VERB: if chosen && !infinitiveShaped(chosen) -> canonical = 'CandidateIsNotCanonical'. Headword shape is optional 'sich ' + one lowercase word ending in -en/-ln/-rn, or sein/tun.
   - Idiom/Collocation: if chosen && source !== 'stored' && judged verbForm is Fin or Part -> 'CandidateIsNotCanonical'.

3. On Idiom/Collocation only, start executeGeneration(generateCanonicalForm, input {markedContext, members, route, needed.canonicalForm, canonicalFormPolicy}, dependsOn []) in parallel with the features judge on a child AbortSignal. Use it when the answer is NotCanonical and no member needs generation; abort it otherwise.

Generator fixes the data requires before shipping:
   (a) VERB targets with a participle member: rank the participle's infinitive first and do not offer finite bekommen/erhalten/kriegen (recipient passive). Found on erhielt überreicht.
   (b) Do not emit rule-derived infinitives for short unknown stems (mus->musen, sol->solen). Better, require a table or stored-Lemma hit for bare single-syllable forms, because jev picks any offered candidate.
   (c) One ellipsis convention per route. Either align the ADP gold ('...') with CCONJ ('…') or emit the route's notation only.

Do NOT include:
   - speculative Luna on VERB/ADJ (0 uses in 186 calls; pure cost);
   - a jev probability threshold (does not separate right from wrong picks);
   - the policy wording change (untested);
   - lowercase single-member or ellipsis candidates for phrasemes;
   - any change to the policy text describing alternatives (support answers are sensitive to policy text).

Follow-up worth one experiment: use the parallel Luna text as an asynchronous verifier. Accept the code pick immediately and re-judge only on disagreement. In this data, agreement had 0 errors in 157 cases, and disagreement caught every wrong VERB code pick.

1) Detach the join; this is the change that fixes click latency. In app/tf-demo/server/linguisticOrchestration.ts:550-566, stop doing `yield* Fiber.join(knowledgeDraft)` for decision === "New". Commit with persistResolvedClick right away, passing a `knowledgeDraftPending: true` flag in place of knowledgeDraftJson. scheduleKnowledgeGeneration (persistence.ts:~672) then inserts the attempt as AwaitingDraft and schedules a safety runKnowledgeGeneration with runAfter of about 10-20 s. After the commit, the same action joins the fiber and calls a new idempotent internal mutation, attachKnowledgeDraft({attemptKey, knowledgeDraftJson}). That mutation patches the attempt and schedules generation now if the attempt is still AwaitingDraft. Keep the Matched-Reading poll/interrupt path as it is. This follows the audit's detach-draft-join design; my data only supplies the latency case for it.

2) Add a per-call timeout in src/concrete-lang/de/knowledge-production/draft.ts: pass `signal: AbortSignal.any([signal, AbortSignal.timeout(6000)])` into each executeGeneration. Check `signal.throwIfAborted()` against the outer signal only, so a timeout falls into the existing KnowledgeDraftFailed -> [] path and produceKnowledge's fallback regenerates the text. Once detached, this bounds time-to-definition; before detaching, it bounds the click. (6 s is above the observed max of 4.5 s for non-stalled calls.)

3) Separately, and only after a manual review on polysemy and multiword cases: change the translation clause in senseTextPrompt (draft.ts:32) and the produce.ts fallback to a citation-form instruction. The D wording in drafts.ts citationTranslationLine gave 0/47 non-citation for both EN and RU in one rep, against 23%/21% in production.

4) Optional, as a cost and time-to-definition change and not a latency fix: merge definition and translations into one sense call and keep transcription as its own call (arm C shape: 42% cheaper, draft p50 -176 ms). Do it only with per-leaf validation and a quality gate. C produced a meaning-inverting definition (kennen -> 'mit niemandem vertraut sein') and more neighbour bleed on the Kakao sentence. Consider adding the transcription line's 'headword, not the sentence form' framing, which seemed to help B's citation behaviour.

Do NOT include:
- the all-in-one bundle with transcription (B): slower p50 (+120 ms) and all-or-nothing nulls;
- a hedged duplicate request for drafts: no p50/p90 gain, 2-10% extra calls;
- any bundling of text into the emoji call;
- a claim that merging removes drafts from click latency: the draft stays the long pole in about a third of New-Reading clicks.
